Chains: SDD

Purpose:
  Define how engineering work moves through dependent engineering stages,
  from task creation to final verification. This is the WHEN/ORDER layer
  (see skills/skills.sdd -> Purpose for the full four-layer map:
  project/architecture/chains/skills).

  Chains exist because a single requested stage is rarely the whole
  unit of engineering work. AI MUST NOT treat completing one stage as
  completing the feature — the chain continues until every required
  downstream stage, including VR, has passed (see Rules [C19] below).
  This is the core reason this file exists, not an incidental rule.


Owns: ./

Scope:
  Chain model
  Stage model
  Stage dependency rules
  Chain transition rules
  Chain validation
  Failure recovery
  Parallel execution rules


DoesNotOwn:
  Project-specific flows
  Project-specific dependencies
  Project-specific tasks
  Engineering skill implementations
  Project architecture

  (see architecture/architecture.sdd -> SeparationOfConcerns — HOW work
  is implemented belongs to skills/; WHICH flow a real project uses
  belongs to project/*/flows.sdd, not here. See Templates below for the
  exact boundary between this engine and a project's own flow.)


Principle:
  A chain represents the execution order of engineering work.
  A chain MUST be dependency-driven, not hardcoded per chain type.
  A chain MUST NOT assume every project uses the same engineering stages
  — see MobileExample below.


Stages:

  Engineering discipline stages — each has one responsibility and one
  dedicated definition file under ./stages/, reusable across every chain
  type and every project (see NoDuplication):

    DB:  Database         — schema/data changes.                  @./stages/DB.sdd
    BE:  Backend           — backend implementation.                @./stages/BE.sdd
    API: API / Contract    — API contract and integration.          @./stages/API.sdd
    FE:  Frontend          — web frontend implementation.           @./stages/FE.sdd
    MD:  Mobile            — mobile implementation.                 @./stages/MD.sdd
    QA:  Quality Assurance — testing and quality validation.        @./stages/QA.sdd
    DO:  DevOps            — build, deployment, infra validation.   @./stages/DO.sdd
    VR:  Verification      — final system verification.             @./stages/VR.sdd

  Chain-orchestration stages — analysis/decision points that begin or
  redirect a chain. These are chain-type-specific, not reusable
  engineering disciplines, so they are defined inline inside each
  template under ./templates/, not given a file here:

    AN:     Analysis         — initial scope/requirement analysis.
    AR:     Architecture     — architecture decisions for new work.
    IMPACT: Impact Analysis  — determine what a bug or change actually
                               affects before touching it. Its exact
                               meaning differs by chain type (bug scope
                               vs. change invalidation) — see
                               templates/bugfix.sdd and templates/change.sdd.


Important:
  VR = VERIFY.
  VR is the final validation stage of a chain.
  VR MUST NOT be treated as an implementation stage.


ChainLifecycle:
  DISCOVER > PLAN > EXECUTE > REVIEW > VERIFY

  Note:
    This is the macro lifecycle a chain moves through as a whole — not
    the same thing as a chain's concrete Sequence (see templates/*.sdd)
    and not the same thing as a single task's own lifecycle (see
    tasks/tasks.sdd -> Lifecycle, which is per-task, not per-chain).


ExecutionModel:
  Chain > Resolve stages > Resolve dependencies > Resolve required skills
  > Execute eligible stage > Validate stage > Continue chain


Dependency:
  requires:    stage cannot execute until the dependency is satisfied
  blocks:      an unresolved stage prevents a dependent stage from starting
  optional:    a stage may be skipped once proven unnecessary (never
               silently — see Rules [C7]/[C9])
  parallel:    stages may execute concurrently when independent
  invalidates: a change requires downstream reevaluation (see Rules [C15])


DynamicFlow:
  Prompt > ImpactAnalysis > DetermineAffectedStages > ResolveDependencies
  > BuildChain > Execute

  Note:
    This is how a chain is actually assembled for a real prompt — stages
    are not read off a fixed list, they are derived. See Templates below
    for where the derivation stops being generic (this file) and starts
    being project-specific (project/*/flows.sdd).


Illustrations:

  MobileExample:
    If mobile is not affected: BE > API > FE > QA > DO > VR
    If mobile is affected:     BE > API > FE > MD > QA > DO > VR

  ParallelExample:
    If DB and BE are independent of each other:
         DB
          |
          +------+
          |      |
          v      v
         BE     API
          |      |
          +--+---+
             |
             v
             FE


Failure:
  Stage > FAILED > ImpactAnalysis > ResponsibleStage > RECOVER > ContinueChain

ReviewFailure:
  Stage > REVIEW > FAILED > ResponsibleStage > RECOVER

VerificationFailure:
  VR > FAILED > ImpactAnalysis > AffectedStage(s) > RECOVER > QA > DO > VR


Completion:
  A chain is DONE only when:
    all required stages are completed
    all required reviews have passed
    all required dependencies are satisfied
    VR has passed


Rules:

  [C1]  Every executable task MUST belong to a chain.
  [C2]  Every chain MUST define its stages as an ordered Sequence (see
        templates/*.sdd).
  [C3]  Every stage MUST have a declared responsibility (see Stages above).
  [C4]  Stage order MUST be derived from dependencies, not hardcoded
        identically for every chain type (see Dependency above).
  [C5]  A stage MUST NOT execute before its required dependencies are
        satisfied.
  [C6]  A stage MAY execute in parallel with another stage when their
        dependencies allow it (see ParallelExample).
  [C7]  A chain MUST NOT silently skip a required stage.
  [C8]  Optional stages MUST declare their activation condition (see
        Dependency -> optional).
  [C9]  A stage MAY be dynamically added when impact analysis identifies
        a new requirement, and MAY be removed when proven irrelevant —
        never silently (see [C7]).
  [C10] Completing one stage MUST NOT automatically complete the entire
        chain.
  [C11] Chain completion requires VR to pass (see Completion above).
  [C12] A failed stage MUST return to its responsible stage for recovery
        (see Failure above).
  [C13] A failed review MUST reopen the responsible stage, not just the
        review step (see ReviewFailure above).
  [C14] VR failure MUST trigger impact analysis before recovery (see
        VerificationFailure above).
  [C15] A completed stage MUST be reopened when a later change
        invalidates its output (see Dependency -> invalidates).
  [C16] Chain definitions MUST NOT contain engineering implementation
        rules (see architecture/architecture.sdd -> SeparationOfConcerns
        — HOW belongs to skills/, not here).
  [C17] Chain stages MAY reference skills.
  [C18] Chain stages MAY reference project models.
  [C19] AI MUST preserve chain continuity — completing the originally
        requested stage MUST NOT be treated as completing the feature
        when downstream stages remain (see Purpose above; this is the
        rule that gives this file its reason to exist).
  [C20] Chain decisions MUST be traceable.
  [C21] Global (engine-level) chain changes require human approval (see
        PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision); project-specific chain/flow
        definitions belong to the project model, not here (see
        DoesNotOwn above).


Templates:
  feature -> ./templates/feature.sdd
  bugfix  -> ./templates/bugfix.sdd
  change  -> ./templates/change.sdd

  Note:
    A template is a generic, project-agnostic stage sequence for one
    chain TYPE (see Rules [C2]). It is still not a real project flow —
    a real project's actual flow (which stages really apply, and in
    what real order for THAT project) is owned by that project's own
    model, e.g. .sdd/project/payments/flows.sdd, not by a template here
    (see DoesNotOwn). A template answers "how does a feature chain work
    in general"; a project flow answers "which flow does THIS project
    actually have" (see Navigation below).


Navigation:
  Stage definitions: @./stages/
  Chain templates:   @./templates/
  Project flows:     @../project/*/flows.sdd
  Tasks:             @../tasks/
  Skills:            @../skills/
  State:             @../state/


State: +

Note:
  (prompt/new/33.md, STEP 11) Restructured from the flat STEP 5 layout
  (chains.sdd router + feature.chain/bugfix.chain/change.chain, Rules
  [C1]-[C11]) into an engine with two subdirectories:

    ./stages/    — one file per reusable engineering-discipline stage
                   (BE/API/FE/MD/DB/QA/DO/VR), extracted out of the three
                   chain files so their responsibility is defined once,
                   not repeated per chain type (see NoDuplication).
    ./templates/ — one file per chain type (feature/bugfix/change),
                   superseding ../feature.chain, ../bugfix.chain and
                   ../change.chain, which are deleted in this same pass
                   (preserved in git history, same precedent as STEP 5's
                   deletion of the old _TEMPLATE.feature.sdd).

  Deliberate departures from the source spec (prompt/new/33.md), applied
  per the standing "apply, don't copy" rule:

  Header kept as `Chains: SDD`, not switched to the source's proposed
  `Spec: ChainSystem` — matches the confirmed repo-wide `<Name>: SDD`
  convention (see state/state.sdd's own Note for the same precedent).

  Source's `Owns:` topic list renamed to `Scope:`, and a real `Owns: ./`
  path line added — same collision and same fix already applied to
  decisions.sdd and state.sdd (`Owns:` is reserved for the directory
  path only).

  Source's separate `StandardStages` and `StageResponsibilities`
  sections were merged into one `Stages:` section (name + responsibility
  + file pointer together) to avoid restating the same eight stages
  twice. AN/AR/IMPACT — present in the existing chain files but absent
  from the source's eight-stage list — were kept and explicitly
  classified as "chain-orchestration stages" (chain-type-specific,
  defined inline in templates/, no dedicated file), rather than dropped;
  the source did not address them at all.

  Source's `Rules [C1]-[C18]` were merged with this file's own prior
  `[C1]-[C11]` (renumbered as one consolidated `[C1]-[C21]` list) rather
  than appended — several source rules restated an existing rule in
  different words (e.g. source [C5] and prior [C3] both express "no
  execution before dependencies"); the clearer wording was kept once.

  Source's generic `Example` section was dropped — it would have
  duplicated templates/feature.sdd's own Sequence (see NoDuplication);
  `MobileExample`/`ParallelExample` were kept under a new `Illustrations:`
  heading because they illustrate the Dependency vocabulary itself, not
  one project's flow.

  Source's `DefaultFeatureFlow` was not embedded here — its content now
  lives once, in templates/feature.sdd -> Sequence, referenced through
  the new `Templates:` section instead of duplicated.

  Old `Resolution:` section (pointing at ./*.chain) renamed `Templates:`
  and repointed at ./templates/*.sdd; its explanatory Note was rewritten
  to state the ENGINE-vs-MODEL boundary the source itself argued for:
  "Global SDD deyir 'flow necə işləyir'. Project flow deyir 'bu
  project-də hansı flow var'" (source, closing section) — i.e. this file
  defines how a chain works in general; a real project's actual flow is
  owned by project/*/flows.sdd, which does not exist in this repo yet
  and is intentionally not created here (see .sdd-only scope — flows.sdd
  is real per-project content, out of bounds until a concrete project
  domain exists under .sdd/project/).
